This is version 1.1 of the macbinary file translator.  This versionfixes some rather destructive bugs in the original.New features include:  1.  Now writes a 128 byte header instead of 129  2.  Uses a 2k buffer for the file copy  3.  Will correctly decode files that have the same filename in themacbinary header.  4.  The new version will correct for characters that the macintoshallows in filenames, but OS9 does not.  (macbin converts spaces to '_',and '-' to '.')The following are the docs for the original version:This an OS9-68000 binary file, I hope to have the OS9/Level 2 soon.If you have ever tried sending a text file from a Macintosh to OS9/68000 with ZMODEM, you probably have experienced one of thefollowing problems:1.  The Mac believes it is sending to a UNIX host, therefore itconverts all CR's to LF's, the OS9 computer believes it is receivingfrom a PC and simply strips LF's.  This process leaves no end of linecharacter.2.  Since ZMODEM uses MS-DOS file name conventions (8 character,3extension) you often lose the actual file name.  Since both the Mac andOS9 can have filenames with 29 characters, this is waste.Sending a file from OS9 to the Mac only causes filename problems. This program converts files into a format called MacBinary.  This allowsthe Mac to believe it is receiving from another Mac, therefore it usesthe filename in the MacBinary header.MacBinary:The Macintosh operating system stores all files as two forks.  The onewith the actual data is the Data Fork, and the other is the ResourceFork.  Because of this the Macintosh community has developed a standardcalled macbinary to merge these two forks into one file for transferbetween computers using standard protocals (xmodem,ymodem ...)  Moreinfo can be found on the Macintosh SIG on DelphiThis program is very simple to use.  type "macbin -?" to get a summaryof options, the program will create a file(s) with the input file plusthe ".MAC" extension.  This allows you to simply type "sz *.MAC" andyour mac will receive it (note you must be using Zterm or anotherprogram on the Mac with ZMODEM support), otherwise you must use anotherprotocal.Here are some popular Macintosh file types for the -t option:TEXT           text fileFSSD           sound filePNTG           macpaint pictureThis program is freeware.  You may use it as you see fit.  However, do makesure my name is included in the archive (preferably this file)  Notethis is the first version, I plan to add support for MACBINARY II andmore options.        Thanks,        Bryan ClingmanIf you have any questions or comments send me mail to:Delphi :  BRYANCCIS    :  76207.3146